  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Water is a mirror after dark. Whatever you light beyond a pool appears twice, and whatever glares across it glares twice as well. The most useful <strong>landscape lighting ideas for pools</strong> start from that fact: light the backdrop, keep sources low and shielded on the deck, and treat the underwater lights as one layer among several. Austin Landscape Lighting has lit pools from Lakeway hillsides to small Mueller courtyards, and the same principles hold at every size.</p>

<h2>Underwater lights are not the lighting plan</h2>
<p>Most Austin pools come with one or two LED niche lights. They make the water glow, which is beautiful and also the reason the rest of the yard looks black by comparison. The landscape system's job is to balance that glow: lit trees and walls beyond the water, a softly lit deck, and clear light on steps and transitions. When the balance is right you can turn the pool light to a low blue or white and the yard still works.</p>

<h2>Idea 1: Light the far side first</h2>
<p>Stand where you sit most, usually the patio, and look across the pool. Whatever is on the far side is your backdrop: a fence, a limestone wall, a row of cypress, a slope with oaks. Uplight it generously. A wall takes wash lights every 8 to 10 feet; a tree line takes one or two uplights per tree with a 36 to 60 degree beam at 2700K. The reflection in the water doubles the effect and makes a small pool look twice as deep. Our <a href="/landscape-lighting-ideas-for-trees/">tree lighting guide</a> covers species-specific placement.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/landscape-lighting-ideas-for-pools-2.webp" alt="Pool with lit trees and stone wall reflected in the water" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>Everything lit beyond the water appears twice; that is where the fixtures go.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Idea 2: Keep the deck glare-free</h2>
<p>Wet coping and travertine throw light straight into your eyes. Rules for the deck:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Path lights stay at least 3 feet back from the water's edge and use full shrouds so you see the pool of light, not the lamp.</li>
  <li>Prefer hardscape lights tucked under seat walls, planter caps and the edges of raised spas. They light the surface from the side, below eye level.</li>
  <li>Step lights go in every riser between the house, the deck and the lawn. Wet steps are the most common pool-area injury and the cheapest thing to light.</li>
  <li>No floods on the house pointed at the pool. Ever.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Idea 3: Spas, spillways and water features</h2>
<p>A raised spa with a spillway wants a light below the water sheet so the falling water glitters, which is usually a small submersible or a hardscape light aimed across the fall. Bubblers and fountains do the same with an under-mounted LED. Keep these at the same color temperature as the rest of the yard; a cool-white fountain in a warm scene looks like a mistake. The <a href="/services/pool-and-water-feature-lighting/">pool and water feature service</a> page lists the fixture types we use for each.</p>

<h2>Idea 4: Pergolas, cabanas and outdoor kitchens</h2>
<p>Structures around the pool need task light that does not spill onto the water. Downlights in the pergola beams aimed at the table, under-counter strips on the outdoor kitchen, and a dimmed sconce or two on the cabana. Put these on their own zone so the kitchen can be bright while the pool area stays low and relaxed.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/landscape-lighting-ideas-for-pools-3.webp" alt="Pool cabana and patio with layered landscape lighting in Austin" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>Pergola downlights for the table, hardscape lights for the deck, trees for the backdrop.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Idea 5: Planting around the pool</h2>
<p>Palms, agaves and ornamental grasses are the usual pool plants and all of them take light well. Palms get a single narrow 15-degree uplight straight up the trunk. Agave and yucca are lit from the side so the leaves cast shadows. Grasses glow when backlit, so put the fixture behind them relative to where you sit. Three or four lit plants are enough; the water does the rest.</p>

<h2>Idea 6: Scenes and controls</h2>
<p>A pool yard has more moods than any other part of the property: swim mode with the deck bright, dinner mode with the water low and the trees up, late mode with only the step lights. A <a href="/services/smart-lighting-systems/">smart controller</a> with three or four zones lets you save those as scenes. If you keep a simple timer, at least put the safety circuit (steps, path) on a photocell so it never goes off while someone is out there.</p>

<h2>Sample pool lighting plan</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Fixtures</th><th>Placement</th><th>Zone</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Backdrop trees</td><td>4 to 6 uplights</td><td>Far side of pool, 36° to 60° beams</td><td>Trees</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Wall or fence</td><td>3 to 5 wash lights</td><td>Grazing, 8 to 10 ft apart</td><td>Trees</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Deck and seat walls</td><td>6 to 8 hardscape lights</td><td>Under caps, below eye level</td><td>Deck</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Steps and transitions</td><td>4 to 6 step lights</td><td>Every riser</td><td>Safety (photocell)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pergola or kitchen</td><td>3 to 4 downlights</td><td>In beams, aimed at table</td><td>Dining</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pool plants</td><td>3 to 4 accents</td><td>Side-lit or backlit</td><td>Deck</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>That is 23 to 33 fixtures on four zones, a planning range of about $7,000 to $13,000 installed depending on fixture grade and controls. It is a planning range, not a quote; the <a href="/tools/cost-estimator/">cost estimator</a> adjusts for your choices.</p>

<h2>Safety and code notes</h2>
<p>Low-voltage landscape fixtures are not underwater fixtures and must not be installed inside the pool or spa. The National Electrical Code keeps 12-volt landscape fixtures and their wiring a set distance from the water's edge, and the transformer must be on a GFCI-protected outlet well away from the pool. We route wire around the deck, not under it, and keep fixtures outside the required clearance. Underwater niche lights stay with the pool contractor and their own bonding and permits.</p>

<h2>Light your pool properly</h2>
<p>Pools are where a design-first approach pays off most, because the reflection doubles every good decision and every bad one. Austin Landscape Lighting will walk your pool yard at dusk, demo fixtures on the far side and give you a written plan with zones. <a href="/contact/">Book a free consultation</a> and get the water working for you.</p>
